NAME
       passwd - change a user's password

SYNOPSIS
       passwd [-k] [-l] [-u [-f]] [-d] [-e] [-n DAYS] [-x DAYS] [-w DAYS]
              [-i DAYS] [-S] [--stdin] [USERNAME]

DESCRIPTION
       passwd sets the password of a user account. Run with no USERNAME it
       changes your own password: it asks for the current one first, then
       the new one twice. root can give a USERNAME, is not asked for the old
       password, and may set any password (a weak one gets a "BAD PASSWORD"
       warning, but root's choice is kept). Ordinary users must pick a
       password that passes the quality checks of pam_pwquality (by default
       at least 8 characters, not a dictionary word, not based on the user
       name).

       The password is stored as a hash in /etc/shadow, which only root can
       read. passwd also changes the password aging fields there, and can
       lock and unlock accounts. The same aging settings can be changed with
       chage, which has more options and a clearer listing (chage -l).

OPTIONS
       --stdin
              Read the new password from standard input (root only). Handy in
              scripts:  echo 'Secret123' | passwd --stdin USER

       -l, --lock
              Lock the password: a "!!" is put in front of the hash, so no
              password can match. Other ways in, such as an SSH key, still
              work. Root only.

       -u, --unlock
              Remove the lock again. passwd refuses when that would leave an
              empty password; -f forces it.

       -d, --delete
              Remove the password, so the account has none. Root only.

       -e, --expire
              Expire the password now: the user must choose a new one at the
              next login. Root only.

       -n, --minimum DAYS
              The minimum number of days between password changes.

       -x, --maximum DAYS
              The maximum number of days a password stays valid.

       -w, --warning DAYS
              How many days before expiry the user is warned.

       -i, --inactive DAYS
              How many days after expiry the account still accepts the old
              password (to change it) before it is disabled.

       -S, --status
              Print a short status line: name, state (PS = password set,
              LK = locked, NP = no password), the date of the last change,
              then the minimum, maximum, warning and inactive days.

       -k, --keep-tokens
              Change only expired passwords.

EXAMPLES
       Change your own password (as student):

              $ passwd
              Changing password for user student.
              Current password:
              New password:
              Retype new password:
              passwd: all authentication tokens updated successfully.

       As root, set another user's password, then in a script:

              # passwd harry
              # echo 'Shine2Day!' | passwd --stdin harry

       Make harry change his password at the next login, and require a new
       password every 90 days:

              # passwd -e harry
              # passwd -x 90 harry

       Lock an account, check it, unlock it:

              # passwd -l harry
              # passwd -S harry
              harry LK 2026-03-02 0 99999 7 -1 (Password locked.)
              # passwd -u harry

FILES
       /etc/shadow
              The password hashes and aging fields.

       /etc/login.defs
              Defaults for new accounts (PASS_MAX_DAYS, PASS_MIN_DAYS,
              PASS_WARN_AGE); these do not change existing users.

       /etc/security/pwquality.conf
              The password quality rules.

SEE ALSO
       chage(1), chpasswd(8), useradd(8), usermod(8), shadow(5),
       login.defs(5)
